          "derivation": "OP θah- (Nourai transliterates the theta as 'th') is the Old Persian continuant of PIE *ḱens-: PIE palatal *ḱ > Proto-Indo-Iranian *ć > Iranian *s, but in the Persid dialect of the Achaemenid inscriptions the cluster *ns/*nh before a vowel yields θ, giving 3sg. θātiy < *θa(n)hati (Pokorny 566 writes exactly \"apers. θ-ātiy ‚spricht, verkündet‘ (*θa(n)hati)\"). θātiy is the single most frequent verb form in the corpus, the formula θātiy Dārayavauš xšāyaθiya 'says Darius the king' that opens nearly every paragraph of Bīsotūn (DB 1.3, 1.6, 1.8f …) and of DNa/DNb/XPh. Kent's Lexicon p.188 lemmatises the root as θah- and glosses it 'declare, say', equating it with Av. saŋh-, Skt. śāsati and Lat. censet, from pIE *kens-. Both the words and the language label are correct.",
