          "derivation": "The root attribution is right, the immediate parent is not. NP سخن soxan continues MP saxwan (MacKenzie: \"saxwan [MPYA < A mly'; shwn' | M sxwn, N suxan] word, speech\"), from Old Iranian *sax(u)an- 'utterance', a -van-/-uan- noun to the verb sah- of nodes #1/#3 — Wiktionary derives the Persian word from Proto-Iranian *cánhati and gives Skt. śáṃsati and Lat. cēnseō as cognates, i.e. exactly PIE *ḱens-. But Hübschmann, Persische Studien §99 (p.210) states the rule and the consequence explicitly: \"Ap. θ im Anlaut vor Vokalen = iran. s = idg. ḱ ist im Np. nicht durch h sondern durch s vertreten … suxun, suxan 'Wort' aus iran. *saxvan- = ap. *θa(h)uvan- von θah 'sprechen', skr. śaṃs-, zd. saŋh-\", and again at §108 (p.218) \"suxun, suxan 'Wort', älter saxwan … aus phl. saxwan\". In other words the New Persian word cannot descend from the θ-dialect form θah- drawn as its parent; it comes through a Middle Persian *saxwan whose s- points to a non-Persid (Median-type) Old Iranian form of the same root. The chart's own NOTE offers \"Kâs 2\" (Nourai's PIE *ḱās- 'to direct, command', p.244) as an alternative parent; modern scholarship keeps soxan with *ḱens- (Hübschmann, Wiktionary), while Av. sāsnā- 'teaching' and sāstar- 'ruler' belong to the other root — the two are frequently confused in Iranian, which is why Nourai hedged.",
